      "answer": "We have evidence of a recurring Canada approval-bypass pattern in two customers: MapleBridge and MapleFork.\n\nMapleBridge experienced about 117 policy approvals bypassing the Canada-specific approver after their migration on 2026-03-12. The root cause was competing rule sets ('canada.v2' and 'global.v3') with identical priority keys combined with delayed schema registry propagation. This caused some rule-evaluation pods to apply global rules instead of Canada-specific ones, leading to automated approvals bypassing required manual Canada approvers. Mitigations included publishing a high-priority override rule 'canada.block-automate-2026-03-15' to route approvals to manual approvers, enabling audit logs for evidence, and planning a hotfix to adjust rule precedence to prefer explicit country overrides over global defaults [art_54e35e2cb431, art_39c1434aa40a, art_e697b3abe158].\n\nMapleFork also had a Canada approval-bypass issue related to region-specific policy precedence. The mitigation involved deploying a temporary manual approval role and adjusting rule evaluation precedence to prioritize region overrides before global defaults, as documented in a detailed playbook [art_ad58f3ce1afd].\n\nNo other customers besides MapleBridge and MapleFork are documented with a recurring Canada approval-bypass pattern in the provided evidence. There is no explicit confirmation whether MapleBridge's issue is a one-off or part of a broader recurring pattern across multiple accounts.\n\nIn plain English, the shared failure pattern is that after migrations or policy updates, the system's rules engine sometimes applies global approval rules instead of the intended Canada-specific rules due to timing delays in schema updates and conflicting rule priorities. This causes automated approvals to bypass the manual approval steps required for Canada, risking compliance issues. The fix involves enforcing rule precedence so that country-specific rules always override global ones and adding manual approval overrides during the fix rollout.\n\nTherefore, MapleBridge and MapleFork are the two customers with documented Canada approval-bypass patterns, sharing a failure pattern of rule precedence conflicts and schema propagation delays causing bypass of required Canada manual approvals [art_54e35e2cb431, art_ad58f3ce1afd, art_e697b3abe158].",
